gy because it is so ingenious  sometimes it leads to delightful whimsy  sometimes it leads to hotmail drafts  folders full of firmware updates and  stuff but sometimes it can lead to some  real social change  i grew up in africa  i spent my childhood in zimbabwe i was  born in kenya we used to go on holidays  when i was a kid where we'd get in the  car and we would just drive for three or  four hours down roads that look like  this you know no civilization no tarmac  no street lights nothing and uh every  once in a while you'd see in the  distance there'd be a little hut by the  side of the road somewhere and it was  always painted bright red and white  hand-painted coca-cola logo and uh  there'd be someone in the i don't know  how but they always had like a crate of  ice-cold coca-cola and uh i went back to  africa a couple years back for my  cousin's wedding i was in zambia and  there were bits of it that haven't  changed in 20 years except the red and  white hand-painted signs don't say  coca-cola anymore they say vodacom  because uh since the turn of the  millennium africa has experienced a  communications revolution trying to run  regular telephone networks across africa  was never gonna work because you need  three things to do that one you need a  dense population of people two those  people need to have a lot of money and  three the government has to have your  back if someone turns up in the night  and steals all of your wire for various  complicated reasons  africa is very sparsely populated a lot  of the people who make up the population  are not terribly affluent they don't  have a huge amount of disposable income  and uh government corruption is rife  across parts of southern and sub-saharan  africa which means you spend a fortune  laying copper cable along the side of  the road you come back in the morning  the cable is gone because someone has  taken it and sold it for scrap and so  for whatever reason africa didn't take  off until cellular networking came along  because mobile phones are a game changer  you don't need the infrastructure you  can put a bunch of masts up on top of a  hill and suddenly three or four hundred  people can be reached by that one cell  phone tower  if you went here this is nairobi capital  city of kenya if you'd visited here  around about the year 2000 uh you know  nairobi is a modern city restaurants you  know banks entrepreneurship there are  even a couple of tech startups running  out of there as early as 2000 but you  could drive a couple of miles outside of  nairobi and you'd find people who had  never made a phone call let alone seen a  computer people who had no idea what the  internet was or why it would matter to  them so as they started rolling out  cellular networking and putting in the  infrastructure one of the challenges  that they had there was how do you roll  out mobile phone networks to people who  don't have bank accounts  eighty percent of the population in  kenya  pardon me is what was known as unbanked  they don't have any kind of banking no  savings nothing they live cash in hand  and what kind of happened is each  village somebody would get a mobile  phone and effectively they'll become  like a little start-up business for them  they'd have a nokia phone and if you  need to make a call you'd go and see  them  you'd give them a little little bit of  cash they'd let you make a call and when  they got enough cash they would get on  their bicycle and they'd go into town  and they'd buy a vodafone top-up card or  a vodacom top-up club they'd buy credit  everything was pre-pay because nobody  had a bank account then the users  realized  that the thing you needed wasn't  actually the top-up card it was the code  the serial number that would activate  the credit and this spawned something  remarkable because a lot of places the  kids would uh you know grown-up kids  would go and work in the cities and  their parents would be way out in the  country in the villages and so someone  would get a job in nairobi they'd go  they'd be working in a factory working  in a bar or something and previously to  send money home either they would have  to make the trip themselves take them  about four or five days  or they would put the money in an  envelope and try and find somebody they  trusted who was going in the right  direction and hope it got there but when  they figured out that they could  transfer mobile phone credit they would  ring the telephone person in their  parents village and they'd be like uh  hey i got 10 000 shillings of top up for  you and they'd read the number out  so they take their cash from their job  they'd buy the card they'd read the  number at guy back in the village or a  lady back in the village would top up  their phone with 10 000 shillings and  the next day when mom comes in for  groceries they'll be like hey  your son sent me 8 000 shillings minus a  you know consideration because you've  got to grease the wheels of commerce  but this was a game changer because it  meant there was suddenly a reliable way  of transferring money that didn't  involve carrying cash now  shortly after that they launched a  friends and family system which meant  you could use a text message to send top  up from your phone to someone else's  phone now this isn't telephone banking  this is literally just transferring  mobile phone minutes of call time credit  but suddenly people started using this  as a form of currency they would you  know go into the store they'd buy some  groceries they would pay by sending a  text message with 500 shillings of  credit store keeper would go out for  beer after work they'd buy their beer  with credit next day the guy running the  bar needs to get his bicycle fixed he  goes to see the place that fixes  bicycles sends him a text message  there's the credit it starts circulating  people are using this as a de facto form  of currency now  this is the point where vodacom might  have gone  this was a terrible idea we launched  mobile phones in kenya and no one's  making any phone calls you know this is  not what we designed the system for but  that's not what they did what they did  instead  was they went there's a really  interesting opportunity here maybe we  can actually take what is already  happening here that you know this has  been created by end users innovating  with the tools we gave them and turn  this into something that we can actually  use and so the product that they created  was a thing called pesa mobile payments  m for mobile uh pacer is the swahili  word meaning money and pacer had a  network of authorized agents so there'd  be someone in your village who if you  needed to you could take your and pacer  in and you could cash out you could get  proper cash back but most people didn't  they just circulated and pacer airtime  credit and this became the de facto  currency of the entire kenyan economy  within about two or three years it has  represented  i saw recently i set a path out of  poverty for something like a quarter of  a million people just because they had a  reliable way of receiving money and  hanging onto it that didn't involve cash  now this only worked in kenya they tried  to roll it out in afghanistan a few  years later didn't take off they tried  it in romania they tried it in india  pakistan tanzania it's still there's a  couple of places in africa ghana they  use it a little bit tanzania they use it  a little bit in uganda it's still  available in kenya it is ubiquitous  trying to shut down pacer in kenya now  would be like telling europe they're not  allowed to use mastercard anymore it's  that relevant and it only exists  because the users used the product wrong  and the managers of the product company  went this is interesting and they didn't  say no we're taking your phones away  they didn't shut the system down they  didn't change the terms and conditions  they learned from what they hadn't  anticipated they looked at something  which could have been construed to be  failure and when actually this is really  interesting there's something here we  could not have anticipated so  go out there you know build the systems  that you build do good work write tests  make sure stuff works cover everything  you can think of but don't ever make the  mistake of thinking that you know better  than your own users because once stuff  goes into production it gets out there  real people start using it failure is  always going to be an option thank you  we have a we have a couple of minutes  for questions folks does anyone have any  questions  if not i know that the next thing on the  program after this is the the party  there will be beers there will be snacks  uh there will be comedy assuming that my  uh co-hosts have finished writing it  well i've been up here talking about  mobile phones and space rockets um stick  around folks enjoy the rest of the  evening enjoy tomorrow if you're not  staying this evening i will see some of  you later on at the party have a great  time  you
 

